it's not often that your home nation  sells where you live to another country  and by extension  you with it but this is what happened to  the creoles living in french louisiana  when it was sold to the united states in  1803.  suddenly going from being a french  subject to living in america must have  been pretty odd which raises the  question  how exactly did the creoles react to the  louisiana purchase  before we begin it's necessary to  discuss the word creole  nowadays it has a racial connotation to  it but it's important to note that this  wasn't the case until the mid-1880s  and as of the louisiana purchase creole  just meant francophone person born in  the new world and said that's what we  use here  so when jefferson purchased louisiana  from napoleon it was to put it mildly  sparsely populated  the non-native american population there  was about 60 000 people  over half of these people were slaves  and of the rest the major groups were  french spanish german and irish settlers  now whilst louisiana was sold by france  it had only belonged to france for about  two years at this point and totally in  secret  they had gotten it from spain who in  turn had won it from france in the seven  years war 40 years prior  and napoleon had been negotiating the  sale before he'd even sent a governor  there to  you know run the place as such you can  see that the people there weren't  exactly attached to the idea of french  sovereignty  since the only difference was that for  about a year there had been french  troops in the region instead of spanish  ones and then all of a sudden those left  and it was american troops in their  place  and even when it was under spanish  control it was done with a light touch  and so any changes in leadership had  little effect on those living there  especially those in rural areas  so how did the people feel about all of  this well the response was mixed  you see the united states didn't make  this land a state but a territory  this meant those who lived there got the  negatives of government like taxes but  were none of the positives like  democracy or citizenship  instead they got the potential future  maybe promise of one day prospectively  becoming citizens  perhaps for most of new orleans and st  louis's populations  they were furious and many took to the  streets to protest their sail and more  importantly they're being forced to  become americans  now with these changes also came  opportunities particularly with respect  to the mississippi river and trade  the us government wanted the river to  become central to continental travel and  commerce  and so its leaders pledged to make it  more navigable and also rid it of its  piracy problem  and so locals in new orleans knew that  their city would likely become a major  trade hub which it did  however this opportunity brought many  american settlers to the city and they  had no interest whatsoever in mixing  with the creoles already there  as for the rural settlers of louisiana  they weren't so keen  for them their recession to the united  states could only mean one thing  new all-american neighbours which meant  more competition and importantly worse  relations with native americans  whom under spanish and french rule had  largely been left alone and if they were  provoked by these new owners it could  lead to retaliation which wouldn't be  directed at those in washington but  those living on the frontier  irrespective of what they thought the  deal was already done  and the louisiana territory would  forever be incorporated into the united  states  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a special  thanks to my patrons  james bissonette mr wolfe kelly  moneymaker sky chappelle rob d  martin captain cydog marvin cassell mo  aaron the white adam stalter jordan  longley boogerly wuggley gustav swan  jerry lamdin rasheed ali gareth turner  maggie patzkowski coppertone winston k  wood spinning three plates corso wolff  marcus asner spencer lightfoot matthew  shipley the mugwopper alex schwinn  lil brepantisi robert wetzel anthony  beckett charles the first and scottish  trekkie
 

